    "body": "Historic mill at Premier gold project Credit: Cambria Gold Mines\nPosted By: Henry Lazenby October 19, 2026\nHigh-grade drill results at Cambria Gold Mines’ (TSXV: CAMB; US-OTC: CAMVF) Silver Coin bolster its plans to secure ore for the restart next year of the idled Premier mill in northwestern British Columbia.\nHole SC26-2714 cut 8.2 metres grading 98.56 grams gold per tonne from 73.7 metres downhole, including 1 metre at 794 grams, the company reported Monday. Silver Coin lies 5 km north of the mill near Stewart, about 890 km northwest of Vancouver.\n“High gold grades over potentially underground bulk mineable widths at the Silver Coin Deposit makes this area a priority for underground development in the spring of 2027,” President and CEO Robert McLeod said in a release.\nSecuring enough mineable feed is central to Cambria’s plan to revive the 2,500-tonne-per-day plant after insufficient underground material derailed its earlier production ramp-up. The company plans to blend material from Silver Coin and Big Missouri with higher-grade ore from the Premier deposit while it develops Red Mountain. Cambria says $538 million (US$377.3 million) has been spent since 2021.\nThe assays strengthen Silver Coin’s potential role as an early source of mill feed, Canaccord Genuity said in a Monday research brief.\nShares were little changed at 71¢ around midday Monday in Toronto. Shares have touched 60¢ and $3.50 apiece over the past 12 months. It has a market capitalization of about $300 million.\nGrade checks\nThe headline interval’s estimated true width is about 5.6 metres, or 68% of its drilled length. The included metre of exceptionally high-grade material accounts for much of the overall grade. True widths across the reported intersections range from 68% to 97% of downhole lengths, according to the company.\nHole SC26-2710 returned 4.4 metres grading 22.62 grams from 91.6 metres downhole and a separate 20 metres at 3.75 grams from 123 metres. Monday’s results cover four holes totalling 657 metres, the first assays released from this year’s Silver Coin infill program.\nCambria expects results from more than 14,000 metres of drilling at the deposit over the next several weeks. The assays are to inform an updated resource estimate and feasibility study targeted for the first half of next year. Infill drilling is expected to continue into 2027, with another round of underground drilling planned early next year.\nRestart hurdles\nCambria is targeting mill commissioning late next year, followed by a production ramp-up through 2028, according to its Oct. 5 update . The Vancouver-based company adopted its name in February after restructuring under its former Ascot Resources banner.\nThe revised approach draws on Silver Coin’s mining history. Westmin and Tenajon Resources mined the deposit’s northern portion in the 1990s. Cambria is using operational information from that work to help model the southern extension, where the latest drilling is focused.\nAccess could come through existing underground workings at Big Missouri, 700 metres north of Silver Coin, or a new portal closer to the mineralized zones, depending on permitting, the company said early this month.\nThe restart still requires plant upgrades, mine authorizations and work on water treatment. Cambria said earlier this month it was moving closer to meeting zinc discharge limits after equipment upgrades and changes to treatment-plant operations.\nAt Red Mountain, the company plans to build the alpine section of its access road next year. It ultimately intends to truck material about 50 km to Premier through Stewart and Hyder, Alaska.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: Cambria Gold Mines\nRepublish this article",
